    "answer_text": "In the copper–silver galvanic cell, the anode is the copper electrode, where oxidation occurs (Cu to Cu2+) [ev_002]. The cathode is the silver electrode, where reduction occurs (Ag+ to Ag) [ev_002]. The spontaneous reaction produces Cu2+ cations in the anode half-cell and consumes Ag+ ions in the cathode half-cell [ev_001]. The source text does not explicitly state the direction of electron flow through the external wire, so I cannot confirm that direction from the supplied evidence; it is not established in these passages. The salt bridge is a tube filled with inert electrolyte solution that connects the two half-cell solutions, keeping the reactants separate while maintaining charge balance [ev_001]. As the reaction proceeds, NO3− flows in from the salt bridge to balance increasing Cu2+ in the anode half-cell, and Na+ flows into the cathode half-cell to compensate for decreasing Ag+ [ev_001]. Regarding charge carriers, the supplied evidence describes ions moving through the salt bridge but does not describe the charge carriers in the external wire, so that comparison cannot be completed from the current evidence.",
